UNIQ(1)                        P32W Unix                         UNIQ(1)

NAME
       uniq - report repeated lines in a file

SYNOPSIS
       uniq [-cdu] [input [output]]

DESCRIPTION
       uniq copies the input file (or the standard input, if none is
       named or it is -) to the output file (or the standard
       output), writing only one copy of each run of identical
       adjacent lines. Repeated lines must be next to each other to
       be found, so the input is usually sorted first. Lines may be
       of any length; a last line without a newline is given one.

OPTIONS
       Options may be combined (-cd) or given separately.

       -c     precede each line with the number of times it
              occurred, in a 4-column field and a space
       -d     write only the lines that were repeated (one copy)
       -u     write only the lines that were not repeated

EXAMPLES
       sort words | uniq             each word once
       sort words | uniq -c | sort -rn
                                     the commonest words first
       sort names | uniq -d          names that occur more than once

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for a usage error, or if a file could not be opened or
       written.

SEE ALSO
       sort(1), grep(1)

BUGS
       There are no -f or -s options (nor V7's -n and +n) to skip
       fields or characters. -d and -u together write nothing.
